        "math_academy_id": "q-347619",
        "problem": "Given that $r(t)=\u27e8{e}^{3t}-4,\\,6\\sqrt{t}\u27e9,$ what is ${r}^{\u2032}(t)?$\n\n${r}^{\u2032}(t)=\u27e8${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$\u27e9$",
        "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)={e}^{3t}-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=3{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=6\\sqrt{t},$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(t)=\\frac{6}{2\\sqrt{t}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8{x}^{\u2032}(t),\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e83{e}^{3t},\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t}}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
